<h2 id="deadline">The 2026 Cutdown Deadline</h2>
<p>The NFL's 53-man roster limit locks in at 6 p.m. ET on August 30, with waiver claims following the next afternoon and 16-man practice squads filling out shortly after. The final preseason weekend — August 27 through 29 — is the last real audition for anyone whose spot isn't already secure. None of that process runs through the combine. But when two players are otherwise close on tape, teams frequently lean on the measurable stuff — long speed, explosiveness, size relative to position — to break the tie. That's the lens here: not who will get cut, but what the testing numbers say about the traits each of these players brought into the league.</p>
<div class="callout">
<strong>Why This Matters</strong>
A player's combine number doesn't expire. It's a snapshot of the physical tools a team drafted or signed for in the first place — useful context when camp reports start using words like "buried on the depth chart" or "clearly on the bubble."
</div>
<h2 id="johnson">Kaleb Johnson, RB, Pittsburgh Steelers</h2>
<p>Johnson enters his second season behind a crowded Steelers backfield, and his path to a full-time role runs through special teams value and receiving work as much as carries. At the 2025 combine, the former Iowa workhorse measured 6'0", 225 lbs and ran an <strong>official 4.57-second 40-yard dash</strong> with a 1.62 10-yard split — solid, not blazing, for the position. That profile fits the tape: Johnson is a between-the-tackles runner with good contact balance rather than a home-run breakaway threat, and scouting reports flagged his straight-line speed as the one trait that lagged behind the rest of his game.</p>
<div class="rank-bar-row">
<div class="rank-bar-label"><span>40-Yard Dash vs. RB Average (~4.52s)</span><span>4.57s</span></div>
<div class="rank-bar-track"><div class="rank-bar-fill" style="width:68%"></div></div>
</div>
<h2 id="jones">Broderick Jones, OT, Pittsburgh Steelers</h2>
<p>Jones was a 2023 first-round pick who's now fighting to hold his starting left tackle job after spinal fusion surgery, with rookie Max Iheanachor pushing for the same reps. His combine profile is the reason Pittsburgh drafted him 14th overall in the first place: at 6'5", 311 lbs, Jones ran a <strong>4.97-second 40</strong> — the fastest time among offensive tackles at the 2023 combine — with a 30-inch vertical and a 1.74 10-yard split. That's rare timed speed for a player his size, and it's exactly why "struggled in camp" reads differently for Jones than it would for a tackle without that athletic ceiling; the traits are still there even when the technique slips.</p>
<div class="rank-bar-row">
