<h2 id="braswell">Chris Braswell, Edge, Tampa Bay Buccaneers</h2>
<p>Tampa Bay has stacked its edge room with first-round rookie Rueben Bain Jr., YaYa Diaby, and Anthony Nelson, squeezing Braswell's path to snaps. His combine testing suggests the talent is real even if the depth chart is crowded: at 6'3", 251 lbs, Braswell ran a <strong>4.60-second 40</strong> with an excellent 1.59 10-yard split, a 33.5-inch vertical, and a 9'7" broad jump. He was also clocked at 21.9 mph in-season on Bruce Feldman's "Freaks List" and reportedly squatted 705 pounds — the kind of power-speed combination teams covet at the position, which is why a release would likely draw waiver interest fast.</p>
<div class="rank-bar-row">
<div class="rank-bar-label"><span>10-Yard Split vs. Edge Average (~1.65s)</span><span>1.59s</span></div>
<div class="rank-bar-track"><div class="rank-bar-fill" style="width:88%"></div></div>
</div>
<h2 id="obj">Odell Beckham Jr., WR, New York Giants</h2>
<p>Beckham's path back to his original team is a legacy story as much as a roster one — he's now 33 and coming off a season with 550 receiving yards in six years, fighting for snaps behind Malik Nabers, Darnell Mooney, and a deep receiver room. It's a sharp contrast to his combine résumé: at 5'11", 198 lbs, Beckham ran a <strong>4.43-second 40</strong> with a 38.5-inch vertical and a 10'2" broad jump back in 2014, numbers that helped make him a first-round pick and, briefly, one of the most explosive receivers in the league. The testing profile hasn't changed — the fact that a decade of NFL wear has passed since it was recorded has.</p>
<div class="rank-bar-row">
<div class="rank-bar-label"><span>Vertical Jump vs. WR Average (~35.5")</span><span>38.5"</span></div>
<div class="rank-bar-track"><div class="rank-bar-fill" style="width:91%"></div></div>
</div>
<h2 id="levis">Will Levis, QB, Tennessee Titans</h2>
<p>Levis sits behind the Titans' quarterback plans as a veteran arm rather than a lock, with the roster crunch at the position always a real possibility this time of year. He skipped the 40-yard dash at his 2023 combine, but his explosiveness numbers stood out for the position: at 6'4", 229 lbs, Levis posted a <strong>34-inch vertical jump</strong> and a 10'4" broad jump — the second-best broad jump among quarterbacks that year, behind only Anthony Richardson. His athletic testing score reportedly ranked fifth among the QB class, evidence of a big, mobile frame even without an official sprint time on record.</p>
