<p>Combine numbers are a snapshot, not a guarantee — plenty of players with modest testing numbers carve out long careers on technique and instincts, and plenty of workout warriors never stick. But when a roster decision comes down to a coin flip between two similar players, teams tend to trust the tape they can measure. That's the case for all five names above heading into Sunday's deadline.</p>
<h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">When is the 2026 NFL roster cut deadline?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Teams must reduce their rosters to 53 players by 6 p.m. ET on Sunday, August 30, 2026. Waiver claims follow at 1 p.m. ET on August 31, with practice squads (16 spots per team) filling out shortly after.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">Do combine numbers actually predict who gets cut?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Not directly. Roster decisions weigh scheme fit, special teams value, contract and cap implications, and live game tape far more heavily than testing numbers. Combine data is most useful as a tiebreaker signal — a way to explain why a team still values a player's ceiling even when camp reporting looks negative.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">What happens to players who get cut?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Released players pass through waivers, where any team can claim them in reverse order of the prior season's standings. If unclaimed, they become free agents and can sign with any team, including returning to their original club's practice squad.</div>
</div>
